import Applied.Statistics.ConditionalError

/-!
# Conditional error principle; sample-size increase with the conventional test

- id: stat-conditional-error-promising-zone
- area: statistics
- status: formalized
- source: M. A. Proschan, S. A. Hunsberger, "Designed extension of studies based on conditional power", Biometrics 51 (1995); H.-H. Müller, H. Schäfer, "Adaptive group sequential designs for clinical trials", Biometrics 57 (2001), and "A general statistical principle for changing a design any time during the course of a trial", Stat. Med. 23 (2004) (conditional error principle); Y. H. J. Chen, D. L. DeMets, K. K. G. Lan, "Increasing the sample size when the unblinded interim result is promising", Stat. Med. 23 (2004); P. Gao, J. H. Ware, C. Mehta, J. Biopharm. Stat. 18 (2008); C. R. Mehta, S. J. Pocock, Stat. Med. 30 (2011) (conventional test in the promising zone); L. Cui, H. M. J. Hung, S.-J. Wang, Biometrics 55 (1999); W. Lehmacher, G. Wassmer, Biometrics 55 (1999) (pre-planned weights). Journal data confirmed at Crossref (for Proschan–Hunsberger via the reference list of P. Bauer et al., Stat. Med. 35 (2016)); only abstracts were read, so the places of the statements in these papers (theorem and equation numbers) are to be confirmed
- used-in: unblinded sample-size re-estimation in confirmatory clinical trials; the demonstration of type I error control that FDA's adaptive-design guidance (2019) and the ICH E20 draft ask for
- prerequisites: independence of σ-algebras generated by disjoint blocks of observations, product measures (Tonelli), Mathlib's real Gaussian distribution, `Applied/Statistics/GroupSequentialCanonical.lean`
- formalized-elsewhere: not found in the Lean repositories searched (NEXT-TARGETS.md, 25 Nov 2026)
- statement-review: unreviewed

Scope of the name. The words "promising zone" in the id of this entry (ids are stable and are
not renamed) and in some alias names below refer ONLY to the region where conditional power
under the current trend is at least 50%, `z₁ ≥ c √(n₁ / (n₁ + n₂))`. The sample-size-increase
theorem (5) is the result of Chen, DeMets and Lan (2004). It is NOT the full promising zone of
Mehta and Pocock (2011) / Gao, Ware and Mehta (2008), whose lower end lies below 50%
conditional power (roughly 0.3 to 0.4, depending on the interim fraction and on the maximal
sample size); that lower part is not covered. Nor is it proved that the type I error is
inflated when the sample size is increased outside the region: only the pointwise strict
decrease of the critical value (4) is proved.

Informal statement.

(1) Conditional error principle, set form (`conditional_error_principle`). `P` is any measure
on `Ω`, `m1` a sub-σ-algebra (the interim information), `D : Ω → ι` a map into a countable
type whose fibres `{D = d}` are `m1`-measurable (the chosen design), `E` any set (rejection by
the original test) and `E' d` any sets (rejection by the replacement test of design `d`).
Hypothesis: for every `d` and every `m1`-measurable `B ⊆ {D = d}`,
`P (B ∩ E' d) ≤ P (B ∩ E)`. Conclusion: `P (⋃ d, {D = d} ∩ E' d) ≤ P E`. The version
`conditional_error_principle_eq` assumes the `E' d` measurable and
`P ({D = d} ∩ E' d) = P ({D = d} ∩ E)` for every `d`, and concludes equality.

(2) Independence as a Fubini formula (`measure_inter_of_independent_second_stage`). `P` a
finite measure, `X` an `m1`-measurable real function, `Y` a measurable real function
independent of `m1`, `B ∈ m1`, `S ⊆ ℝ × ℝ` measurable. Then
`P (B ∩ {(X, Y) ∈ S}) = ∫⁻ ω in B, (law of Y) {y | (X ω, y) ∈ S} ∂P`.

Gaussian two-stage model for (3)–(7): `P` is a probability measure, `ξ 0, ξ 1, …` are
measurable, mutually independent, each with the standard normal law (so the null hypothesis
holds and the variance is known). `S n = ξ 0 + … + ξ (n-1)`, `Z n = S n / √n`. The first stage
is the first `n1` observations, `F1 = firstStage ξ n1` is the σ-algebra they generate, and a
second stage of size `d` is the next `d` observations `ξ n1, …, ξ (n1 + d - 1)`.
`critValue n1 c z m = (c √(n1 + m) - z √n1) / √m` (real `m`) and
`condError n1 c z m = N(0,1) [critValue n1 c z m, ∞)`, which equals
`1 - Φ(critValue n1 c z m)` (`conditional_error_eq_one_sub_cdf`).

(3) Conditional error function (`ztest_conditional_error_function`). For `n1 > 0`, `d > 0`,
any real `c` and any `B ∈ F1`:
`P (B ∩ {Z (n1 + d) ≥ c}) = ∫⁻ ω in B, condError n1 c (Z n1 ω) d ∂P`.

(4) Calculus facts, for a natural number `n1` and reals `c ≥ 0`, `z`, `0 < n2`:
`critValue_le_of_promising`: if `n2 ≤ m` and `z ≥ c √(n1 / (n1 + n2))` then
`critValue n1 c z n2 ≤ critValue n1 c z m`. `critValue_lt_of_unpromising`: if `n1 > 0`,
`n2 < m` and `z < c √(n1 / (n1 + m))` then `critValue n1 c z m < critValue n1 c z n2`.
`critValue_monotoneOn_iff`: if `n1 > 0`, `m ↦ critValue n1 c z m` is nondecreasing on the real
half-line `[n2, ∞)` if and only if `z ≥ c √(n1 / (n1 + n2))`.
`promising_zone_iff_trend_conditional_power`: for `n1 > 0`, `n2 > 0`, any `c`, `z`:
`critValue n1 c z n2 - (z / √n1) √n2 ≤ 0` if and only if `z ≥ c √(n1 / (n1 + n2))` (the
left-hand side is the argument of `1 - Φ` in the conditional power under the drift `z / √n1`
per observation, so this says "conditional power under the current trend at least 1/2"; the
conditional power itself is not defined in Lean).

(5) Sample-size increase with the conventional test, Chen–DeMets–Lan (2004): increase only
when conditional power under the current trend is at least 50%
(`conventional_test_promising_zone_type_one_error`). `n1 > 0`, `n2 > 0`, `c ≥ 0`.
`N2 : Ω → ℕ` is `F1`-measurable, `N2 ω ≥ n2` for every `ω`, and `N2 ω ≠ n2` implies
`Z n1 ω ≥ c √(n1 / (n1 + n2))`, for every `ω` (not just almost every). Then
`P {ω | Z (n1 + N2 ω) ω ≥ c} ≤ N(0,1) [c, ∞)`. Here `Z (n1 + N2 ω)` is the ordinary z-statistic
of all `n1 + N2 ω` observations, which equals `(√n1 Z₁ + √N2 Z₂*) / √(n1 + N2)`.
`conventional_test_promising_zone_type_one_error_of_zStat` is the special case where the
second-stage size is a measurable function `ℝ → ℕ` of `Z n1`.

(6) Level of the fixed design (`ztest_fixed_design_level`): for `n > 0` and any `c`,
`P {Z n ≥ c} = N(0,1) [c, ∞)`.

(7) Pre-planned weights (`weighted_test_exact_level`). `n1 > 0`, `n2 > 0`, any real `c`,
`N2 : Ω → ℕ` `F1`-measurable with `N2 ω > 0` for every `ω` (increases and decreases both
allowed, no zone restriction). With `Z₂ = stageTwoZ ξ n1 (N2 ω) ω` the z-statistic of the
`N2 ω` second-stage observations,
`P {(√n1 Z n1 + √n2 Z₂) / √(n1 + n2) ≥ c} = N(0,1) [c, ∞)`. For `N2 = n2` the statistic is
`Z (n1 + n2)` (`weighted_statistic_eq_zStat`, needs only `n2 > 0`). This is the inverse-normal
combination test with weights `√(n_i / (n1 + n2))`; it is proved here directly from (1)–(3),
not through a general combination-test theorem.

Differences from the textbook statement. The principle (1) is stated for events and
inequalities of probabilities on `m1`-measurable sets, not for test functions and conditional
expectations `E[φ | F1]`; (2) and (3) identify the conditional error function in the same set
form (they say that the integrand is a version of the conditional probability, without using
Mathlib's `condExp`). Randomised tests are not covered. The design space must be countable.
Levels are stated as `N(0,1) [c, ∞)`, an extended nonnegative real, not as `α` with
`c = z_{1-α}`; the hypothesis `c ≥ 0` in (5) corresponds to `α ≤ 1/2`. Sample sizes are numbers
of unit-variance observations in a one-sample model; the two-arm case with equal allocation
reduces to it but that reduction is not formalised. In (5) the second-stage size may depend on
all first-stage data, not only on `Z n1`, and no upper bound on it is needed. The published
rules restrict increases to a zone that also has an upper end and a maximal sample
size; for the Chen–DeMets–Lan result only the lower end `z ≥ c √(n1 / (n1 + n2))` (conditional
power under the current trend at least 50%) matters for the type I error bound and only it is
assumed. The Mehta–Pocock promising zone starts at a lower value of `z` that depends on the
maximal sample size; increases for `z` between that value and `c √(n1 / (n1 + n2))` are NOT
covered by (5). The "exactly when" in (4) is for real-valued second-stage sizes; for integer
sizes only the sufficiency direction is used.

Not covered: unknown variance (t-tests) and nuisance parameters; a group-sequential first
stage or early stopping for efficacy or futility; decreases of the sample size with the
conventional test; that the bound in (5) can fail outside the region `z ≥ c √(n1 / (n1 + n2))` (only the
pointwise strict decrease of the critical value is proved, not an inflated type I error;
whether the level is inflated depends on the rule); the Mehta–Pocock / Gao–Ware–Mehta
promising zone below 50% conditional power; power, bias of estimates and confidence
intervals after adaptation; non-null drift; the general Müller–Schäfer principle with
conditional expectations of test functions; a concrete probability space is not constructed
here (one is `Applied.GroupSequential.canonicalMeasure` with the coordinate maps).
-/

